**If we use window functions like sum, avg, lag, or lead with only orderBy and without partitionBy, Spark performs a global ordering. To maintain this order, Spark shuffles all data into a single partition, which eliminates parallelism. As a result, only one executor/core handles the computation, leading to performance degradation, high memory usage, and possible job failure for large datasets.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Driver (splits tasks)
     ↓
Cluster Manager (allocates resources)
     ↓
Nodes (machines)
     ↓
Executors (processes)
     ↓
Cores
     ↓
Tasks run

The driver is the main Spark program that splits the job into tasks and coordinates execution. The cluster manager allocates resources across multiple nodes. Executors are launched on these nodes, and the driver assigns tasks to executors, which execute them in parallel using their CPU cores.

Each executor executes multiple tasks in parallel using its allocated CPU cores.

NOTE:
Spark uses executors instead of nodes directly because:
Executors provide controlled, isolated, and efficient execution of tasks using allocated resources (cores & memory).
Node is just a hardware where as Executors are Spark-aware processes -Understand tasks
Execute code
Manage memory
Handle failures

Nodes → provide hardware
Executors → use that hardware intelligently
Spark → interacts with executors (not nodes directly)
