for joins if we use repartition on joining key then same keys in both tables will kept in one partition then shuffling of data will be less so that we can acheive optimization
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
In a broadcast join, the smaller dataset is copied to all partitions of the larger dataset. Each partition of the large dataset performs the join locally using the broadcasted data. If matching keys are found, the joined result is produced, eliminating the need for shuffle and improving performance.

 If both datasets are large, we use repartition() on the join key before joining to reduce shuffle and improve performance.
 If one dataset is small and the other is large, we use broadcast() on the smaller dataset to avoid shuffle completely and perform a faster join.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Repartition can be used as an optimization technique for joins, groupBy, and window functions because all these operations require data with the same key to be processed together. By repartitioning on the key column, we reduce shuffle and improve performance, especially for large datasets.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
We use coalesce() when we want to reduce the number of partitions to avoid unnecessary overhead. It is useful when the dataset becomes smaller after transformations like filter, and also before writing data to control the number of output files.

If my dataset has fewer partitions (e.g., 3) than available CPU cores, I can use repartition() to increase the number of partitions so that more tasks run in parallel and CPU resources are better utilized.
